iqr — Interquartile range                                              data · tier 1 · since 0.5.0 · 1.0 · closed form

USAGE
    data.iqr(var, type: Int = 7)

DESCRIPTION
    The distance between the third and the first quartile, `quantile(var, 0.75) - quantile(var, 0.25)`,
    under the quantile definition `type:` names (the nine definitions of Hyndman and Fan, as `quantile`
    offers them; 7 unless given, R's default). A robust measure of spread: half of the values lie within it,
    and one outlier cannot move it. The quartiles are taken over the values present, with the reducers'
    warning for the rows with `NA` skipped (section 4.1 of the Language Reference); one value gives 0.

ARGUMENTS
    data        Frame. The frame.
    var         Var. The variable.
    type:       Int = 7. The quantile definition, 1 to 9; 7 unless given.

VALUE
    The range, a Float, printed as `mean` prints.

MISSING VALUES
    The two quartiles are taken over the values present, with a warning for the rows skipped. A variable
    with no value left gives `NA`.

DETAILS
    The two quartiles come from `quantile` under one definition and are subtracted; nothing else is
    computed, so the result agrees with `quantile` to the bit.

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    W0201   rows with `NA` in the variable were skipped
    Z0401   `type:` is not 1 to 9, or the variable is not numeric
    Z0101   the variable is not in the frame
    W0205   the quantile falls between Inf and -Inf

COMING FROM R OR STATA
    Equivalents: R IQR().

    Zeff                   R                                     Stata
    d.iqr(wage)            IQR(d$wage, na.rm = TRUE)             summarize wage, detail, then display r(p75) - r(p25)
    d.iqr(wage, type: 6)   IQR(d$wage, na.rm = TRUE, type = 6)   no option of its own

    The definitions are numbered as R numbers them. Stata's `summarize, detail` takes its percentiles by
    another of the nine than R's default, so its figure may differ a little from R's and from this one.

RELATED TASKS
    - For the two quartiles themselves, use `quantile`.
    - For another spread that extreme values do not move, use `mad`.
    - For the standard deviation, use `sd`.

ACCURACY
    Closed form.

REFERENCES
    Hyndman, R. J. and Fan, Y. (1996) Sample quantiles in statistical packages, The American Statistician 50(4), 361–365.
    R Core Team, the `IQR` function of the stats package.

SEE ALSO
    quantile, mad, sd, median

EXAMPLES
    // The distance between the quartiles of the wages: half of the wages lie within it. The warning counts
    // the rows with no wage.
    > let wages = dataset(wages)
    > wages.iqr(wage)
    16.17
    warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 2)

    // Under another definition of the quantile.
    > wages.iqr(wage, type: 6)
    16.175
    warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 3)

    // It is the difference of the two quartiles.
    > wages.quantile(wage, p: 0.75) - wages.quantile(wage, p: 0.25)
    16.17
    warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 4)
    warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 4)
